Dashboard Responsibilities

The dashboard is:

a guided control surface for applying configuration inside Mastodon.

It:
  • displays suggested copy
  • deep-links to the correct Mastodon admin page
  • tracks completion state

It does not:
  • store editable configuration
  • overwrite Mastodon state
  • attempt to stay in sync with Mastodon

⸻

Checklist Item Contract

Each checklist item contains:
  • label
  • purpose text
  • deep link
  • suggested copy (optional)
  • status

Status values
  • suggested → not yet applied
  • applied → user confirmed
  • skipped → user intentionally ignored

⸻

MVP Behavior

Application is manual:

User flow:
  1.  Click Open
  2.  Paste suggested copy in Mastodon
  3.  Save
  4.  Return → click Mark done

We do not read Mastodon state in MVP.

Completion state is user-declared.

⸻

Post-MVP (API-assisted mode)

Some payloads may be auto-applied during provisioning:

Examples:
  • about_text
  • owner profile
  • registrations_mode
  • discovery_mode

Dashboard then shows:
  • ✅ Applied automatically
  • 👁 View suggested copy
  • ✏ Edit in Mastodon

Mastodon remains source of truth.

⸻

Resume Flow

If user returns without purchasing:
  1.  Load Blueprint
  2.  Skip regeneration
  3.  Show purchase step

After purchase:

→ go directly to populated dashboard.

⸻

UX Mental Model

User learns:

Commons = where I find the right button
Mastodon = where I make the change

This:
  • trains Mastodon navigation
  • avoids admin UI replacement
  • keeps system scalable

⸻

State Transitions

Blueprint lifecycle

new → generated → saved → bound_to_instance

Instance lifecycle

none → provisioning → live

Payload lifecycle

suggested → applied | skipped

Domain Binding

When provisioning completes:
  • instance_domain is written to ProvisioningMetadata
  • owner_handle is finalized
  • deep links become active

⸻

Success State

A creator:
  • completes onboarding without provisioning
  • can return at any time
  • purchases
  • lands in a fully prepared dashboard
  • applies configuration in minutes

⸻

Product Definition (Canonical)

The onboarding flow produces a complete, reusable launch configuration before purchase.
Provisioning only creates the server and reveals a guided application dashboard.
Mastodon remains the system of record for all live settings.

